026 fundamentals", "Expert ratings cluster from Tilt D to Safe D, consensus Likely D", "Prediction markets price Gillen around 89-92% to win"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable Democratic margin: a +2 Democratic district, an incumbent with a massive money advantage, and a favorable national environment. Markets and most raters agree, though a few rate it only Lean or Tilt D, so the forecast sits slightly below the baseline at a mid-single-digit-to-low-double-digit Democratic edge.", "watch": "Any late surge of outside spending against Gillen or a shift in the national environment toward Republicans could narrow this into tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Laura Gillen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Laura Gillen defends a Long Island district that leans about two points Democratic, against Republican Jeanine Driscoll, a town official who won her primary in June. Gillen has raised over $5 million and holds $3.8 million on hand, dwarfing Driscoll's roughly $265,000 raised, giving her a dominant advertising position for the fall. No public general-election polling is available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and expert assessments.\n\nRaters span Tilt D to Safe D, with a consensus around Likely D, and prediction markets price Gillen's win chances near 90 percent. The race has drawn some negative coverage, including attacks over a 9/11 health program vote and a disputed tax-hike claim, but nothing suggests a fundamental shift. A Democratic win of roughly ten points is the central expectation, with meaningful residual uncertainty given the absence of polls.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9622, "margin": 11.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9401, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9422, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9272, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2